an. According to the 2011 Census, the island's single village, located on the western shore and facing the towns of Kali and Preko on Ugljan, has a resident population of 29 inhabitants, and its highest point, called Lazaret, peaks at 90 m (300 ft).","wpu":"Ošljak (island)","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/25/O%C5%A1ljak.jpg/960px-O%C5%A1ljak.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lukoran","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":44.1,"lo":15.166667,"d":5026,"ds":"Lukoran is a village in Croatia, located on the Adriatic island of Ugljan overlooking at the Zadar peninsula. It is connected with other island villages with the State route D110. Lukoran was mentioned in the year 1068.","wpu":"Lukoran","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/2/29/Primorje.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Kožino","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":44.166667,"lo":15.2,"d":5871,"ds":"Kožino is a village located 9 km northwest of Zadar, in northern Dalmatia, Croatia, with population of 815 (2011 census). The church of St. Michael the Archangel was built in 1522.","wpu":"Kožino","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b6/Eglise_Saint-Michel_de_Kozino_1.JPG/960px-Eglise_Saint-Michel_de_Kozino_1.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Ugljan","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":44.083333,"lo":15.166667,"d":5975,"ds":"Ugljan is an island in northern Dalmatia, Croatia, and the first in the Zadar Archipelago. It is located northwest of the island of Pašman and southeast of the islands of Rivanj and Sestrunj. Separated from the mainland by the Zadar Channel, it is connected with the island of Pašman by the Ždrelac Bridge spanning over the Ždrelac Strait.","wpu":"Ugljan","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/75/Ugljan_8_from_st_Mihovila.jpg/960px-Ugljan_8_from_st_Mihovila.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Michael Fort","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":44.0716,"lo":15.1608,"d":7195,"ds":"Fort St. Michael (Croatian: Utvrda sv. Mihovila; Italian: Fortezza di San Michele) is a fortress on the island of Ugljan, in northern Dalmatia, Croatia.","wpu":"St. Michael Fort","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/f/f3/UgljanSvMihovil1.jpg/960px-UgljanSvMihovil1.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Kukljica","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":44.035,"lo":15.246389,"d":9276,"ds":"Kukljica is a village on the island of Ugljan in Croatia, and the only settlement in the eponymous municipality. According to the 2011 census, there are 714 inhabitants, 98% which are Croats. The area of Kukljica also includes the islets of Mišnjak, Karantunić, Bisage and Golac with a total area of 6.9 square kilometers.","wpu":"Kukljica","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/82/Kukljica_Municipality.PNG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Zadar (US: ZAH-dar, Croatian: [zâdar] ), historically known as Zara , is the oldest continuously inhabited city in Croatia. It is situated on the Adriatic Sea, at the northwestern part of Ravni Kotari region. Zadar serves as the seat of Zadar County and of the wider northern Dalmatian region.","wpu":"Zadar","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b3/Roman_Forum_in_Zadar%2C_Croatia_%2848607823862%29.jpg/960px-Roman_Forum_in_Zadar%2C_Croatia_%2848607823862%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}